{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfvanTqxGQNoxurG7GY9r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Recommendation: don't remove it yet. Investigate first, because the evidence points to a redesign regression more than to lack of demand.**\n\n**Why**\n- The drop began the week the toggle's screen changed. That is a strong sign the cause is the redesign: the toggle may be harder to find, mislabeled, buried, or broken.\n- Silence in support tickets doesn't mean nobody cares. Users who can't find a setting rarely file tickets. They just stop using it, or quietly churn when they lose signal.\n- A 60% drop would be an unusually steep decline in real demand over one month. A UI change is the likelier cause.\n\n**Questions to answer (analytics only)**\n1. Did the drop come from fewer new enablements, or from existing users losing the setting? A migration that reset the toggle would explain the second.\n2. In the funnel, did settings-page views stay flat while taps on the toggle fell?\n3. Who were the users who relied on offline mode? Check their retention, session value and connectivity profile. Compare the 40% who still use it with those who stopped.\n4. What does keeping it cost? Get an engineering estimate of the maintenance burden. \"Simplify the codebase\" is a real benefit, but it needs a number.\n\n**Next step**\nSpend about a week on the funnel and cohort analysis above. If the toggle is hard to find or broken, fix that and re-measure for 2–4 weeks. Removing the feature is hard to reverse and would hit your most connectivity-dependent users hardest.\n\nDecide on removal only if usage stays low after discoverability is restored and the maintenance cost is material. If it comes to that, deprecate with notice rather than cutting it outright."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3452,"cache_read_input_toke